{#- Atlas — surface three: a record, rendered as the file it already is (decisions 2, 11, 15).

  Atlas is never the record. This page renders one, names where it lives, and links to it on
  GitHub so the reader can reach the authority rather than trust the rendering.

  The link to the file is `blob/HEAD`, never a branch name. GitHub resolves `HEAD` to the
  repository's own default branch, so the link is correct for a `main` consumer and a `master` one
  alike — decision 43 says Atlas does not force a branch model, and `atlas.config.json` gives a
  consumer no way to name theirs.

  The record supplies its own <h1>. This page does not print `doc.title` above it: the title was
  derived FROM that heading, so printing it back would announce the same words twice in a row and
  put the contents list's first entry on the heading of the page you are already reading. Decision
  11 forces the record's own heading; nothing forces a second one, and a duplicate Atlas invented
  for itself is decision 11 inverted. The breadcrumb and the lede establish context instead.

  Data this layout expects:
    doc      — { title, path } — path is the record's location in the repository; `title` names
               the page in <title> and in the records index, not in the body
    record   — the record, already rendered by renderMarkdown. Not `content`: that is one of
               Eleventy's reserved data names, and .eleventy.js keeps that guard switched on
    anchors  — headingAnchors(source), for the contents list
    project, repo, title — see base.njk
-#}
{% extends "base.njk" %}

{% block bodyClass %}page page-document{% endblock %}

{% block content %}
<p class="breadcrumb"><a href="/">Feature planning</a> · <a href="/records/">Records</a></p>
<p class="lede">Rendered from <a href="https://github.com/{{ repo }}/blob/HEAD/{{ doc.path }}"><code>{{ doc.path }}</code></a>. Where this page and that file disagree, the file is right.</p>

{% if anchors | length %}
<nav class="contents" aria-label="Contents">
<span class="meta-label">Contents</span>
<ul>
{% for heading in anchors %}
<li class="level-{{ heading.level }}"><a href="#{{ heading.id }}">{{ heading.text }}</a></li>
{% endfor %}
</ul>
</nav>
{% endif %}

<article class="doc">
{{ record | safe }}
</article>
{% endblock %}
